 "gap_lines_detail": {
  "yield_method": null,
  "yield_basis": "\"winter wheat, spring wheat, lentils\" comes out on top for steady income at today's prices, and for lentils it uses Washington-average harvests, not local ones. It is already too close to call. With those harvests 5% lower, \"winter wheat, spring wheat, barley\" is on top. Here, winter wheat runs 23% above the Washington average, spring wheat 6% above and barley 9% above. If lentils did the same, \"winter wheat, spring wheat, lentils\" would stay on top, though in some cases within $10 an acre of \"winter wheat, spring wheat, barley\".",
  "past_years_test": "The past-years test is not testable here: the model kept \"winter wheat, spring wheat, chickpeas\" in every window, so there was no switch to judge for a rotation grown here.",
  "test_windows": "The 67% of switches that held up in the worst year comes only from starting rotations that no mapped field near the pin has run twice in a row, and from test windows that overlap. It is not a test of advice a farmer here could have followed."
